WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.167
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:10.587
I went to my first AA meeting
when I was seven years old, and

00:00:10.587 --> 00:00:15.641
I know what you guys are thinking,
what the hell was in this girl's

00:00:15.641 --> 00:00:20.140
baby bottle.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:20.850
&gt;&gt; Spoiler alert,

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.870
I was not an alcoholic at age seven.

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:27.380
My uncle took me to that AA meeting
because there are people in my family

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:27.960
who were.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.495
In fact, there are people in my family
suffering from many forms of addiction.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.710
Knowing that addiction would
dramatically affect my life,

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.020
he wanted me to be informed about it.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.950
So he exposed me to addiction,
from the viewpoint of an addict, so

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.250
that I could understand their struggle.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.260
According to the Center of Addiction,

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.190
40 million Americans are suffering
from substance addiction.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.530
This includes alcohol,
illicit drugs and prescription opioids.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:00:59.870
40 million.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.270
That's nearly one in every eight people.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:07.310
Look at your row.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.300
On average,
at least one person is addicted.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.960
138 million Americans

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.618
have a close friend or family member
who is suffering from addiction.

00:01:20.618 --> 00:01:26.080
That's nearly 50% of our entire
country who knows an addict.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:33.250
And yet, due to shame, people that
know an addict never talk about it.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:36.960
They hide it from their friends,
they hide it from their neighbors,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.889
they even hide it from
other family members.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:45.220
People who know an addict go through
great lengths to keep this secret.

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:50.890
I know because up until this talk,
I was one of these people.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:58.250
Drug addiction is defined by the National
Institute for Drug Abuse as a complex,

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:04.840
chronic brain disease characterized
by drug craving, seeking and use.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.780
The key to this craving is dopamine.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:12.510
Dopamine is the neurotransmitter in your
brain that's responsible for pleasure.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.380
Totally natural and necessary function.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.950
Dopamine surges happen when you're
watching a movie you really love, or

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:25.910
even foods that you enjoy,
perhaps chocolate or pasta.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:30.568
Or when you're having sex,
well, hopefully.

00:02:30.568 --> 00:02:33.757
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:33.757 --> 00:02:35.158
&gt;&gt; When you consume drug,

00:02:35.158 --> 00:02:38.942
over time it overstimulates
the dopamine in your system causing

00:02:38.942 --> 00:02:43.510
a euphoric effect that is 2 to 10 times
the amount that your body is used to.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:49.900
This is the high, but over time
when you continue to consume drugs,

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:59.930
the surges in dopamine really make it
difficult to participate in life fully.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:06.360
This is why, when addicts aren't using
drugs, they complain of feeling flat,

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.830
lifeless, and depressed all the time.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:13.630
At this point it stops being
about using drugs to get high and

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:19.322
starts being about using
drugs just to feel normal.

00:03:19.322 --> 00:03:23.644
An addict needs substances to
reach the same levels of dopamine

00:03:23.644 --> 00:03:25.120
as a non-drug user.

00:03:29.303 --> 00:03:33.355
Addiction literally rewires your brain.

00:03:33.355 --> 00:03:36.005
Addiction is not a choice.

00:03:36.005 --> 00:03:37.740
This is literally a brain disease.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.560
So the question then becomes,

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.060
if research has proven that addiction
is not a choice but a brain disease.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.920
How in the United States do
we justify putting addicts in

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.490
prisons instead of treatment centers?

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:58.391
Would we put cancer patients in
prison because they have cancer?

00:03:58.391 --> 00:04:01.991
Of course not.

00:04:01.991 --> 00:04:06.744
65% of prison inmates
are clinically addicted.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:12.590
Many of them were convicted for personal
drug use, not even intent to sell.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.719
We put addicts in prison for
the compulsive use of a drug that their

00:04:17.719 --> 00:04:20.923
brain literally tells them
that they need to survive.

00:04:24.709 --> 00:04:28.776
But this is not just about
an increase in the prison population.

00:04:28.776 --> 00:04:34.082
Addiction cause our economy a lot
of money, $520 billion to be exact,

00:04:34.082 --> 00:04:38.210
this is the cost to our economy
in lost work productivity,

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:43.623
healthcare expenses, criminal justice
cost, and law enforcement cost.

00:04:45.864 --> 00:04:49.509
And the problem is only growing

00:04:51.093 --> 00:04:57.390
Opiod addiction alone has grown
by 493% in the last seven years.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:04.304
The US Surgeon General has declared
opiod addiction a public health crisis

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:07.662
that is both underappreciated and
undertreated.

00:05:12.809 --> 00:05:16.274
Do addicts choose to try these substances?

00:05:16.274 --> 00:05:20.130
Yes, were they coerced into trying them?

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:27.570
Probably not, but does that mean that
they choose an entire life of addiction?

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.130
No, no one grows up and says, I really,

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:34.740
really want to be
an addict when I get older.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:37.560
They just don't.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:42.310
I've spoken to you guys
today about a number of

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:46.440
statistics but
addiction is not statistics.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:50.570
Addiction is the mother
who lies to her family and

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:54.440
friends because she is too ashamed to
admit that her son has a drug problem.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.860
Addiction is the father who
steals from his kids piggy banks

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.340
to feed his drug habit.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:07.837
Addiction is being called by the police
to identify your sister's body,

00:06:07.837 --> 00:06:09.580
because she just overdosed.

00:06:11.259 --> 00:06:13.370
These are not examples that I made up.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:17.640
These are examples from
people that I know,

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.930
some of whom are in this room right now.

00:06:22.779 --> 00:06:26.348
My uncle took me to that AA
meeting when I was seven,

00:06:26.348 --> 00:06:28.847
because he knew what I did not know.

00:06:30.893 --> 00:06:33.060
My mother was addicted to crack.

00:06:35.392 --> 00:06:40.415
Like many addicts, she was unreliable,
she was irresponsible,

00:06:40.415 --> 00:06:44.909
and when I was four,
she stopped caring for me altogether.

00:06:47.577 --> 00:06:52.980
Growing up as a child of an addict is
a very painful and traumatic experience.

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:59.587
You live in secrecy mortified that
people would judge you for this disease.

00:06:59.587 --> 00:07:05.118
So you do everything you
can to keep it hidden.

00:07:05.118 --> 00:07:09.718
When I was little, I used to just think
if I could just get more straight As or

00:07:09.718 --> 00:07:12.650
more awards,
she would want to be my mom again.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:20.154
I didn't understand how my mom could
want a drug more than she wanted me.

00:07:23.269 --> 00:07:26.368
I recently re-met my mom after 23 years.

00:07:30.274 --> 00:07:33.455
People ask me how do
you forgive an addict?

00:07:33.455 --> 00:07:35.480
How do you forgive addiction?

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:41.521
Understanding that this not a choice, but

00:07:41.521 --> 00:07:45.400
literally a brain disease,
is a critical step.

00:07:48.251 --> 00:07:51.355
I wasn't sure how to give this talk today,

00:07:51.355 --> 00:07:54.977
because the topic is so
taboo and so personal, but

00:07:54.977 --> 00:08:01.037
there are literally 40 million Americans
that are suffering from this disease.

00:08:01.037 --> 00:08:05.573
And if we do not start treating this
like the health issue that it really is,

00:08:05.573 --> 00:08:09.317
we are further enabling a disease
that is killing thousands,

00:08:09.317 --> 00:08:13.870
destroying families all over this country,
and has a real economic cost.

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:20.050
We, as a society, have to make a paradigm
shift into how we are addressing

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:26.256
addiction, one that creates more
patients and fewer prisoners.

00:08:26.256 --> 00:08:29.439
Thank you.

00:08:29.439 --> 00:08:33.961
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:33.961 --> 00:08:37.779
[MUSIC]

